@node Gowl Edit Field
@chapter Gowl Edit Field: Edit Any Text Field in Emacs

@cindex edit field
@cindex text field, editing in Emacs
@cindex text-input-v3
@cindex gowl, text fields
@findex cmacs-gowl-edit-field

With the keyboard in a text field of @emph{any} window---a browser's
comment box, a chat client, a form in some settings dialog---one key
pops that field's text into an Emacs buffer.  @kbd{C-c C-c} puts the
edited text back in the field; @kbd{C-c C-k} gives up.  In between, all
of cmacs applies to text that lives in somebody else's program: evil,
snippets, spell checking, the AI region commands.

It needs @command{cmacs --gowl}.  Emacs has to @emph{be} the
compositor, because only the compositor sits between an application and
its input method, and that is where the text is read and written.
Under plain pgtk the command says so and does nothing.

@lisp
(add-hook 'cmacs-gowl-mode-hook #'cmacs-gowl-edit-field-setup)
@end lisp

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-key
@findex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-setup
That binds @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-key} (@kbd{Super+Ctrl+e}) in the
compositor.  Nothing is bound until @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-setup}
is called.  (@kbd{Super+e} is the bar's AI rewrite and
@kbd{Super+Alt+e} is gabbrev's search, hence the chord.)

Three things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{It only ever replaces what it read.}  The edit sent back is
``delete these bytes around the cursor, insert this'', measured against
the text the application reported.  Text Emacs never saw cannot be
damaged by a commit.
@item
@strong{It cannot always see the whole field.}  The protocol caps what
an application reports, and some widgets report less than that.  The
header line says how much you are editing before you type anything.
@item
@strong{It leaves password fields alone.}  A field the application
marks as a password or a PIN is refused; so is every window on
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-deny}.
@end itemize

@section Using it

@enumerate
@item
Put the keyboard in a text field in any window.
@item
Press @kbd{Super+Ctrl+e}.  A small floating Emacs frame opens over that
window with the field's text in it, point where the field's cursor was,
and the field's selection as the region.
@item
Edit.
@item
@kbd{C-c C-c} (or @kbd{C-x #}, as for @code{server-edit}) commits;
@kbd{C-c C-k} aborts.
@end enumerate

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
@itemx C-x #
@findex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-finish
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-finish}: put the text back in the field and
close the editor.
@item C-c C-k
@findex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-abort
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-abort}: close the editor and leave the
field as it was (asks first if there are edits).
@item C-c M-w
@findex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-copy
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-copy}: copy the text to the kill ring and
close---for when it cannot go back.
@item C-c C-w
@findex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fetch-whole
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fetch-whole}: fetch the @emph{whole} field
through the clipboard, replacing the buffer.
@end table

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-major-mode
@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-mode-alist
The buffer is named @file{*edit-field: @var{title}*} after the window.
Its major mode is @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-major-mode}
(@code{text-mode}), unless @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-mode-alist}
names another for the application: by default chat clients get
@code{markdown-mode} when that is installed.
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-mode} is the minor mode that carries the
keys, with the usual hook.

Pressing the key again in a window that is already being edited shows
that editor rather than starting a second one.

@section The editor window

The buffer is shown in a frame of its own, not in a window of the main
frame, and that frame @strong{floats}: the tiled layout does not reflow
when the editor opens and does not reflow when it closes.  Toggling
layouts does not tile it either.

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-frame-name
This is done with a gowl window rule, not from Lisp.  The frame is
named @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-frame-name}
(@samp{cmacs-edit-field}), which is its window title, and before the
frame is made a rule is installed that floats and centres windows with
exactly that title.  Window rules are applied by the
compositor @emph{before} a window's first placement.  The Lisp hook
@code{cmacs-gowl-client-pre-map-functions} cannot do this: it runs on
the Lisp thread, after the window has been placed once, so a window it
floats visibly jumps and the tiles shuffle twice.

Once the window exists the frame is centred over the window it is
editing, kept inside the output's usable area, and given the keyboard.
If the rule did not apply (an old build, or a title the toolkit set
late) the window is floated at that point instead---one visible jump
rather than none.

The rule is reinstalled each time an editor opens, removing any earlier
copy first: @code{cmacs-gowl-float-rules} clears every rule when it is
reapplied.

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-frame-width
Columns (default 100).
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-frame-height
Lines (default 24).
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-frame-minibuffer
@code{t} (the default): a minibuffer of its own.  @code{nil}: the main
frame's.
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-frame-parameters
Extra frame parameters; they win over the defaults, except @code{name}.
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-frame-name
The frame's name and title, which the float rule matches (default
@samp{cmacs-edit-field}).
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-evil-insert
Start in evil's insert state (default @code{t}).
@end table

The frame has no tab bar, tool bar or menu bar, and carries the frame
parameter @code{cmacs-edit-field}, so other code can tell it from a
real frame.

Closing the editor window by hand counts as giving up on the commit,
but not on the text: a buffer with edits in it is kept, and a message
names it.

@section How the text travels

An application with a text field describes it to the compositor over
the @code{text-input-v3} protocol, so that an input method can work:
the text around the cursor, where the cursor is, what is selected.
gowl already relays this between applications and input methods.
Editing a field in Emacs uses the same state:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Reading} (@code{gowl-text-field-read}) takes the surrounding
text the application reported, with the cursor and the other end of the
selection.
@item
@strong{Writing} (@code{gowl-text-field-replace}) answers the way an
input method does: one @code{delete_surrounding_text} reaching both
ways from the cursor and one @code{commit_string}, applied by the
application together.
@end itemize

No keys are typed and the clipboard is not touched.  Any character
arrives intact, whatever the keyboard layout, and the application sees
one edit it can undo as one.

When no input method (fcitx5, ibus) is running, an application's text
input is not active: gowl only enters text inputs when there is an
input method behind them.  For a read or an edit it @emph{borrows} one:
it enters the focused window's text input, the application answers by
enabling it and describing its field, gowl reads or edits, and leaves
again.  An application that has not answered within 250 ms had no text
field with the focus.

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-focus-timeout
Because an edit is measured from the cursor, the commit first gives the
keyboard back to the window being edited and waits for the focus to
land---with the compositor's focus hook and a deadline
(@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-focus-timeout}, one second), never by
blocking.

@subsection Only the difference is sent

The edit is the smallest one that turns the old text into the new: text
the two share at the start and at the end is left alone, as far as the
cursor allows (a deletion is counted from the cursor, so the kept head
cannot reach past it nor the kept tail start before it).  Changing one
word of a long comment sends one word.

@subsection A field that changed while you were editing

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-verify
With @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-verify} (the default) the field is
read again just before it is written.  If it still holds what was read,
the edit is measured from wherever its cursor is @emph{now}.  If it
changed---you typed in it, the page rewrote it---nothing is sent: the
header line says the field changed, and a second @kbd{C-c C-c}
overwrites what is there now, knowingly.

@subsection When the commit does not arrive

If the application does not take the edit, or the window will not take
the keyboard, the editor stays open with the reason in its header line,
and the keyboard comes back to it.  Nothing is lost; @kbd{C-c M-w} puts
the text on the kill ring.

If the window being edited disappears, the buffer is kept, the header
line says the window is gone, and committing offers to copy the text to
the kill ring.

@section How much of the field you get

Two limits, neither of which cmacs can lift:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{The protocol's cap.}  text-input-v3 limits surrounding text to
4000 bytes.  An application with more in its field sends a window
around the cursor.  Text of 3900 bytes or more is therefore treated as
a window, and the header line says @samp{editing the @var{n} bytes
around the cursor, not the whole field}.  Committing replaces that
window and nothing else.
@item
@strong{The widget's choice.}  Text under the cap is ``all the
application chose to send''.  For a single-line entry that is the
field.  Some multi-line widgets report only the paragraph around the
cursor.  The header line then reads @samp{the text the application
reported}: it is sound to edit and commit, it is just not the whole
field.
@end itemize

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-partial-action
In both cases @kbd{C-c C-w} fetches the whole field through the
clipboard instead (see below), and
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-partial-action} set to @code{fallback} does
that automatically for a read that hit the cap.

@section The clipboard fallback

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fallback
Windows with no text-input-v3 at all---X11 programs under Xwayland,
some toolkits---cannot be read as above.  With
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fallback} set to @code{clipboard} (the
default) they are reached this way instead:

@enumerate
@item
The user's clipboard is remembered, and a marker is put on it.
@item
@kbd{Ctrl+A} and @kbd{Ctrl+C} are sent to the window
(@code{gowl-text-field-send-key}: by keysym, so they are those chords
in any keyboard layout).
@item
If the clipboard holds something other than the marker a moment later,
that is the field's text, and the editor opens on it.  The clipboard is
put back.
@item
On commit: the new text goes on the clipboard, @kbd{Ctrl+A} and
@kbd{Ctrl+V} are sent, and the clipboard is put back once the
application has had time to paste.
@end enumerate

It is @strong{best effort}, and the header line says @samp{through the
clipboard (best effort)}:

@itemize @bullet
@item
It trusts the application to treat those three chords the usual way.
@item
The selection is visible in the application, and stays selected while
you edit.
@item
Where the cursor was is not known; point starts at the end of the text.
@item
An empty field cannot be told from ``no text field'': both copy
nothing, and both are reported rather than guessed at.
@item
Whether the paste happened cannot be confirmed.
@item
Text put on the clipboard @emph{from here} is marked transient (the
@code{x-kde-passwordManagerHint} MIME type, via
@code{gowl-clipboard-set-transient}), which gowl's clipboard history
and the kill-ring sync both skip.  The application's own @kbd{Ctrl+C}
is an ordinary copy and does pass through them.
@item
A clipboard that held something other than text (an image) cannot be
put back.
@end itemize

@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fallback-deny
It is @strong{never} used on a terminal, where @kbd{Ctrl+C} interrupts
the running program: @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fallback-deny} lists
terminal app-ids.  Set @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fallback} to
@code{nil} to have no fallback at all.

@section What is never read

@itemize @bullet
@item
@vindex cmacs-gowl-edit-field-deny
Windows matching @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-deny}, a list of regexps
tried against the app-id and the title, ignoring case.  The default
covers password managers (KeePassXC, Bitwarden, 1Password, @dots{}),
password and authentication prompts (pinentry, polkit), the lock
screen, and any window with ``password'' in its title.  The list
applies to the text-input path and to the clipboard fallback alike, and
a denied window is not asked anything.
@item
Fields the application itself marks as secret: content purpose
@code{password} or @code{pin}, or the @code{hidden_text} /
@code{sensitive_data} hint.  This is checked on what the application
reports, whatever the deny list says, and such a field is not passed to
the clipboard fallback either.
@item
Emacs's own frames.  There is nothing to pop out of Emacs into Emacs.
@end itemize

gowl cannot recognise a password field that does not declare itself; a
window with no text-input-v3 says nothing about its fields.  For those
the deny list is the only guard, which is why it errs wide.

@section Options

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-key
The compositor key (default @samp{Super+Ctrl+e}); @code{nil} for none.
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-major-mode
Mode of the editor buffer (default @code{text-mode}).
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-mode-alist
@code{(@var{app-id-regexp} . @var{mode})}; the first match whose mode
exists wins.  By default chat clients get @code{markdown-mode}.
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-deny
Windows never read or written.
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fallback
@code{clipboard} (the default) or @code{nil}.
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-fallback-deny
App-ids the fallback is never used on (terminals).
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-partial-action
A read that hit the cap: edit the @code{window} (the default), or use
the @code{fallback}.
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-verify
Read the field again before writing it (default @code{t}).
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-focus-timeout
Seconds to wait for a window to take the keyboard (default 1.0).
@item cmacs-gowl-edit-field-key-delay
Seconds the fallback gives the application to act on a key (default
0.15).
@end table

@section Primitives

Defined in @file{cmacs/gowl/cmacs-gowl-textfield.c}.  Each takes the
gowl lock for the length of the call.  Offsets and counts are
@strong{bytes of UTF-8}, because that is what the protocol carries;
@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field--byte-to-char} and @code{--char-to-byte}
convert.

@defun gowl-client-has-text-input-p client
Non-@code{nil} if @var{client} has a text input enabled, or one that
can be borrowed (no input method running, @var{client} focused).  The
second is a promise of an attempt, not of a text field.
@end defun

@defun gowl-text-field-read client callback
Read the focused field of @var{client}, which must hold the keyboard.
Return @code{nil} when no text input reaches @var{client}
(@var{callback} is then never called), else @code{sent} or
@code{pending}, and call @var{callback} exactly once, later, with a
plist: @code{:status} (@code{ok}, @code{no-field},
@code{no-surrounding}), @code{:text}, @code{:cursor}, @code{:anchor},
@code{:complete}, @code{:hint}, @code{:purpose}.
@end defun

@defun gowl-text-field-replace client delete-before delete-after text &optional callback
Delete that many bytes before and after the cursor and insert
@var{text}, as one edit.  Return @code{nil}, @code{sent} or
@code{pending} the same way; @var{callback} gets @code{t} when the edit
was delivered and @code{nil} when the application did not take it.
Long @var{text} is sent in pieces.
@end defun

@defun gowl-text-field-send-key client key &optional modifiers
Press and release the key that produces the keysym @var{key} in the
current layout, with @var{modifiers} (@code{control}, @code{shift},
@code{alt}, @code{super}) held.  Synthetic: no keybind sees it.
@end defun

@defun gowl-clipboard-set-transient text
@code{gowl-clipboard-set}, marked ``paste me, do not keep me''.
@end defun

@subsection Threads and callbacks

gowl answers a read or an edit on its dispatch thread, where Lisp must
never run (two threads winding one @code{specpdl} is a crash).  So the
C callback only copies the answer into C strings and queues it for
Emacs's thread with @code{cmacs_glib_invoke_on_main}.  The Lisp
callback therefore always runs later, from the main loop---including
when gowl could answer at once---and never before the primitive
returns.

Between the call and its answer the Lisp callback lives in a
@code{staticpro}'d hash table keyed by an integer; that integer is the
only thing handed to gowl.  A callback leaves the table exactly once:
when its answer is delivered, when gowl refuses the request, or when
the compositor stops (@code{gowl-stop} empties the table, since a
compositor being torn down drops its waiting operations without
answering).  Ids are never reused, so a late answer finds nothing
rather than somebody else's callback.

@section Limitations

@itemize @bullet
@item
The application must speak text-input-v3 for the direct path: GTK 3 and
4, Qt 5 and 6, Firefox, Chromium and Electron with Wayland IME enabled.
Others get the clipboard fallback.
@item
With an input method running, a field is only reachable while the
application has its text input enabled, which is whenever a text field
has the focus.
@item
Only one read or edit waits on a window at a time.
@item
A long commit goes in several messages (a Wayland message is at most
4096 bytes), each after the application has acknowledged the last.  An
application that stops answering part way has part of the text; the
editor stays open and says so.
@item
While an editor frame is open it is one of Emacs's own top-level
windows.  Code that looks for ``the Emacs window'' as the first
non-embedded client (@code{gowl-embed--find-emacs-client}) can find the
editor instead.
@end itemize

